WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.794
Spherical CNNs generalize convolutional networks to
signals living on the sphere,

00:00:05.874 --> 00:00:09.908
which makes them a natural fit for molecules and
weather data.

00:00:09.988 --> 00:00:14.420
But until now they were stuck at low resolutions
and shallow depths,

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.334
so they never really competed on large real-world
problems.

00:00:18.414 --> 00:00:22.580
This paper, Scaling Spherical CNNs from Google
Research and MIT,

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.628
shows how to scale these models by a full order of
magnitude.

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:30.144
With new activations, normalization, residual blocks,

00:00:30.224 --> 00:00:32.466
and a TPU-optimized implementation,

00:00:32.546 --> 00:00:38.305
the authors reach state of the art on the QM9
molecular benchmark and become competitive

00:00:38.385 --> 00:00:40.760
on several weather forecasting tasks.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.958
Spherical CNNs replace the plane with the sphere as
the domain of the signal,

00:00:46.038 --> 00:00:50.156
which is exactly right for data like molecules and
the atmosphere.

00:00:50.236 --> 00:00:54.099
The catch is that their core operation, spherical
convolution,

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:56.643
is most accurate in the spectral domain,

00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:00.905
and that is far more expensive than an ordinary
planar convolution.

00:01:00.985 --> 00:01:04.912
Because of this cost, spherical CNNs had been
limited to small,

00:01:04.992 --> 00:01:08.156
low-resolution problems with modest model capacity.

00:01:08.236 --> 00:01:14.007
There simply was no large-scale spherical
architecture analogous to the deep planar networks

00:01:14.087 --> 00:01:16.170
that power modern computer vision.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:23.699
Two scientific problems motivate this work:
predicting molecular properties and forecasting the weather.

00:01:23.779 --> 00:01:27.522
Both are intrinsically spherical and tied to rotations.

00:01:27.602 --> 00:01:32.039
A molecule's properties don't change when you
rotate it in space,

00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:36.279
and the Earth's atmosphere is naturally a signal on
a sphere.

00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:40.589
Rotation-equivariant spherical CNNs should be a
perfect match.

00:01:40.669 --> 00:01:43.230
But the standard benchmarks are large.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.775
QM9 has one hundred thirty four thousand molecules,

00:01:46.855 --> 00:01:52.683
over eighteen times bigger than the tiny QM7 set
earlier spherical CNNs could handle,

00:01:52.763 --> 00:01:56.089
and weather grids demand high spatial resolution.

00:01:56.169 --> 00:01:58.730
To compete, these models had to scale.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.685
The authors contribute a systematic recipe for
scaling spherical CNNs by an order of magnitude.

00:02:02.765 --> 00:02:06.339
It has three parts.

00:02:06.419 --> 00:02:09.994
First,

00:02:10.074 --> 00:02:13.648
an efficient implementation of spin-weighted
spherical harmonic transforms in JAX,

00:02:13.728 --> 00:02:17.303
tuned to run fast and distributed on TPUs.

00:02:17.383 --> 00:02:20.957
Second,

00:02:21.037 --> 00:02:24.612
new general-purpose layers and activations that
improve both expressivity and efficiency.

00:02:24.692 --> 00:02:28.266
And third,

00:02:28.346 --> 00:02:31.921
application-specific input representations designed
for molecules and for weather data.

00:02:32.001 --> 00:02:35.575
A key finding is that naive scaling, just adding
depth and width, is not enough;

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:39.230
the core components themselves had to be redesigned.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.722
The method builds on spin-weighted spherical CNNs.

00:02:42.802 --> 00:02:47.828
Its centerpiece is a set of new components that all
live in the spectral domain.

00:02:47.908 --> 00:02:53.446
A phase collapse nonlinearity takes the modulus of
the features to collapse their phase,

00:02:53.526 --> 00:02:58.808
which restores rotation invariance while losing no
information in the nonzero spins.

00:02:58.888 --> 00:03:03.403
Batch normalization and pooling are also moved into
the spectral domain,

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:08.893
and the residual block adds its skip connection
directly between Fourier coefficients.

00:03:08.973 --> 00:03:10.617
On the implementation side,

00:03:10.697 --> 00:03:17.702
the authors compute the Fourier transforms as dense
matrix multiplications rather than fast Fourier transforms,

00:03:17.782 --> 00:03:23.830
because on TPUs matrix multiplies are extremely
fast while memory reshuffling is the bottleneck.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.507
The experiments span two very different domains.

00:03:28.587 --> 00:03:32.885
For molecules, the benchmark is QM9, with one
hundred thirty four thousand molecules,

00:03:32.965 --> 00:03:37.262
up to twenty nine atoms each, and twelve regression
targets covering energetic,

00:03:37.342 --> 00:03:41.640
electronic, and thermodynamic properties.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.017
For weather,

00:03:46.097 --> 00:03:50.395
the models are trained on ERA5 reanalysis data
through the WeatherBench benchmark,

00:03:50.475 --> 00:03:54.772
forecasting quantities like geopotential height and
temperature at three and five day horizons,

00:03:54.852 --> 00:03:59.150
plus longer tasks reaching out to twenty eight days
and iterative high-resolution forecasting.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:03.830
The results are strong on both fronts.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.210
On QM9, the scaled spherical CNN reaches state of the art,

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:12.590
beating the previously dominant graph neural
networks and transformers on eight of twelve

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.970
targets under the first data split and nine of
twelve under the second.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:21.350
On weather,

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.730
it outperforms the WeatherBench baseline on every
metric in the simpler two-predictor setting,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:30.110
and it even beats models that were pre-trained on
large amounts of simulated data on several temperature metrics.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.490
This is the first demonstration that spherical CNNs
are viable neural weather models.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.224
A careful ablation isolates the effect of each change.

00:04:38.304 --> 00:04:40.578
Starting from the JAX implementation,

00:04:40.658 --> 00:04:44.267
the phase collapse activation cuts error by eight percent,

00:04:44.347 --> 00:04:48.591
spectral batch normalization trims a further one
and a half percent,

00:04:48.671 --> 00:04:52.662
and the efficient residual block another two and a
half percent,

00:04:52.742 --> 00:04:54.315
all while improving speed.

00:04:54.395 --> 00:04:59.658
A separate comparison confirms that the phase
collapse activation, spectral pooling,

00:04:59.738 --> 00:05:05.827
and the new spherical molecule representation each
beat the prior alternatives from earlier work,

00:05:05.907 --> 00:05:12.060
together driving the QM9 enthalpy error down to
about fifteen point two five milli electron volts.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.547
Here are the numbers that summarize the impact.

00:05:15.627 --> 00:05:21.582
The models scale by one full order of magnitude in
both operations and feature resolution

00:05:21.662 --> 00:05:23.819
compared to prior spherical CNNs.

00:05:23.899 --> 00:05:31.210
They set state of the art on eight of twelve QM9
targets in the first split and nine of twelve in the second.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:36.160
The new JAX implementation is about three times
faster than the original,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:42.059
and running distributed across thirty two TPUs
speeds it up by a hundred times or more.

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:47.280
And the phase collapse nonlinearity alone reduces
QM9 error by eight percent.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:52.002
The takeaway is that spherical CNNs were never
fundamentally limited,

00:05:52.082 --> 00:05:53.861
they were just poorly scaled.

00:05:53.941 --> 00:05:58.412
With a redesign of the nonlinearity, normalization,
and residual block,

00:05:58.492 --> 00:06:01.744
plus an implementation tuned to modern accelerators,

00:06:01.824 --> 00:06:04.564
these models finally scale to real problems,

00:06:04.644 --> 00:06:11.999
reaching state of the art on molecular property
prediction and becoming genuinely competitive neural weather models.

00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:18.280
The authors release their JAX implementation as a
platform for further research on spherical data.
